{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Behold, your days have drawn near, etc.</b> The reason He said ״הֵן״ is to justify Moses' statement when he said ״בֶּן מֵאָה וְעֶשְׂרִים שָׁנָה אָנֹכִי הַיּוֹם״, and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Sotah 13b): ״הַיּוֹם מָלְאוּ יָמַי״ etc. — end of quote. And the word of HaShem came to him, and He said ״הֵן״ — as you said, your days have drawn near. And His saying ״קָרְבוּ יָמֶיךָ״ (plural) and not ״קָרַב יוֹמְךָ״ (singular), follows the pattern of (Genesis 47:29) ״וַיִּקְרְבוּ יְמֵי״ etc., which we explained there." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying ״הֵן קָרְבוּ״ etc. in this manner: behold, your days have drawn near to you — and the days are the portions of the soul that draw near in the sod of ״תֹּסֵף רוּחָם יִגְוָעוּן״ (Psalms 104:29), as we explained there in parashat Vayechi. And He informed him that the reason for the drawing near of the portions of the soul called ״days״ is to die." "<b>And present yourselves, etc.</b> Here He commanded that Joshua conduct himself in authority before Moses. And this is His saying ״וְהִתְיַצְּבוּ״ — an expression of appointment and authority, both of them equally, following what we explained in parashat Atem Nitzavim (Deuteronomy 29:9). And His saying ״וַאֲצַוֶּנּוּ״ is an expression of kingship, following the pattern of (I Samuel 13:14) ״וַיְצַוֵּהוּ ה׳ לְנָגִיד״, and so it says (Deuteronomy 31:23) ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא״, and they of blessed memory interpreted (Sanhedrin 8a): take a staff and strike them on the head."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:56.827570589Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}